the economic situation in the united  states is dire and there's many people  who psychologically have packed it in  and they're preparing for a recession  but the recent bank earnings coming out  of q2 show us exactly how the consumer  is doing on the ground our friends over  at john street capital they broke down  these bank earnings and they have tons  and tons of insights into spending  savings and credit quality trends  if we start with jamie dimon and jp  morgan they say that quote consumers are  in good shape they're spending money  they have more income jobs are plentiful  they're spending 10 more than last year  almost 30 percent more than pre-covet  businesses you talk to them and they're  in good shape we've never seen business  credit be better ever like in our entire  lifetime and that's the current  environment  on top of that if you take the combined  debt and credit spend is up 15  year-over-year also we can see that the  average consumer is spending 35 percent  more year-over-year on gas and about six  percent more on recurring bills and  other non-discretionary categories at  the same time they have yet to observe a  pullback in discretionary spending  including in the lower income segments  with travel and dining growing 34  percent year over year with spending  growing faster than incomes median  deposit balances are down across  different income segments for the first  time since the pandemic started though  those same cash buffers still remain  elevated of course lots of people are  focused on credit and that's still  healthy and what we can see there is  that positive trends in loan growth  across the business with loans up seven  percent year over year and up two  percent quarter over quarter now that  brings us to where we go from here which  is quote the future environment which is  not that far off involves rates going up  maybe more than people think because of  inflation there's a range of potential  outcomes from a soft landing to a hard  landing driven by how much rates go up  the effect of quantitative tightening  etc  this terrible humanitarian crisis in  ukraine and the war and then the effect  of that on food and oil and gas and  we're simply pointing out those things  make the probabilities and possibilities  of these events different it's not going  to change how we run the company the  economy will be bigger in 10 years we're  going to run the company we're going to  serve more clients we're going to open  our branch we're going to invest in the  things and we'll manage through that so  i always remind myself the economy will  be a lot bigger in 10 years  next up if we go take a look at  citigroup earnings we can see that  they're quoting higher rates and  quantitative tightening will keep  volatility high that said while  sentiment has shifted little of the data  i see tells me that the u.s is on the  cusp of a recession  consumer spending remains well above  pre-covet levels with household savings  providing a cushion for future stress  the job market remains very tight you  can see how resilient the customer is in  the us through the elevated payment  rates and the low level of credit losses  they have shifted their spend far more  to travel and entertainment which is now  outpacing 2019 levels when we go when we  take a look at wells fargo we can see  there quote while we're monitoring risks  related to continued high inflation  increasing interest rates and the  slowing economy which will impact our  customers consumers and businesses they  have been resilient so far when looking  at simple averages across our entire  portfolio consumer deposit balances per  account increased from first quarter and  a year ago and they remain above  pre-pandemic level  overall our consumer deposit customers  health indicators including cash flow  payroll and overdraft trends are not  showing elevated risk concerns  however we're closely monitoring  activity by segment for signs of  potential stress for certain cohorts of  customers we have seen average balances  steadily declined to pre-pandemic levels  following the final stimulus payments  early last year and debit card spend has  also been declining for wells fargo  debit card spending is up three percent  with transactions flat credit card  spending is up 28  although consumer credit utilization  rates remain below pre-pandemic levels  and payment rates remain strong with low  delinquency rates if we go take a look  at morgan stanley gorman stated that if  he had to use one word to describe the  environment it would be complicated  we have the russian invasion of the  ukraine obviously a historic occasion we  have historically low rates with very  significant rate increases going around  the world  we've got the tail of covet we've got  the fears of inflation and actual  inflation we've got enormous political  change in the uk supply chain issues  china-u.s relations etc so yes it's very  complicated i think it's important to  say though it is not 2008 complicated  this is a different type of financial  stress in the system frankly the banking  sector is much stronger than it was  going into the last time we went through  a major reset in 07 and 08 while we  might head into some form of recession  and i like many others have tried to  handicap it  we frankly guessing at this stage but i  think it's unlikely to be a deep and  dramatic recession at least in the  united states then if we take a look at  bank of america they said our u.s  consumer clients remained resilient with  continued strong deposit balances and  spending levels loan growth continued  across our franchise and our markets  team helped clients navigate significant  volatility reflecting economic  uncertainty as we enter the second half  of 2022 we believe we are well  positioned to deliver for our  shareholders while continuing to invest  in our people our businesses and  communities we can look at the trends in  their loans and leases according to john  street capital deposits and overall  asset quality along with consumer and  commercial portfolios still continue to  look strong another quote states that  across our 35 million core checking  accounts the us consumer remains quite  resilient consumers continue to spend at  a healthy pace the overall average  deposit balance for most client cohorts  are higher than where they were in the  first quarter and they rose in june  month over month they remain at  multiples versus pre-pandemic levels  customer data shows bank of america  spend at highest quarter ever with 1.2  trillion of total spending which is up  12 percent year over year also the  highest debit spending quarter on record  in june of 2022 spending was plus 11  percent year over year and transactions  were up six percent year over year for  the first two weeks of july spending is  up ten percent and transactions are up  six percent we see shifts on what people  are spending on experiences travel and  fuel and less on retailing in stores in  the second quarter we saw higher gas  spend and continued recovery and travel  and restaurant spending average deposit  balances remain elevated what these  reports from the banks are showing is  that although there's plenty of fear  when it comes to a recession is that the  consumer is still spending money they're  looking for experiences they're looking  to travel and there's some sort of  hangover from the covid lockdowns  regardless of what the economic data  suggests in terms of gdp contraction in  q1 and potentially in q2 many consumers  on the investment side psychologically  have prepared for a recession they've  seen their asset prices fall off a cliff  and they're hurting they're reeling but  the average consumer the people who  actually spend money in the economy to  consume goods and services they're out  there they're still spending and they  don't see a recession on the horizon  regardless of what you think is going to  happen this is a weird situation we've  got 40-year high inflation we've got a  federal reserve that's hiking rates  after gdp contraction in the first  quarter we've got investors who are  preparing for a recession we've got  consumers who are spending like there's  only good times ahead i don't know where  we go from here but what i can tell you  is that there haven't been many times in  history where we've had this concoction  of different ingredients we're both  investors consumers economists and  people at the federal reserve and in the  treasury are all confused they're just  trying to figure it out make sure you  keep paying attention right now there's  a lot of moving parts and the future is  definitely uncertain what you always  should think about is how to prepare  yourself for the worst  but remember optimists win in the long  term so hope for the best  keep a good attitude and my guess is  that we'll get to the other side of this
 

